// IDENTITY-GRAINED FLOOR DISPOSITION RECEIPT (operator ruling 2026-08-19).
//
// Every DECLARED witness identity in the tree gets exactly one `RequiredFloorDisposition`, keyed
// by its qualified `module.function` identity, and the host joins the two populations at that
// grain — `FloorDispositionJoinInexact` refuses a missing, duplicated or foreign row rather than
// reporting counts that add up (DESIGN §5: completeness is an identity join). This is the single authority for that fact: the host loop in
// `v1_compiler.cli_run` `run_required_floor` computes admission by testing a claim's authored
// module name against `long_home_prefixes()` and `fixture_home_prefixes()` below, then REALIZES
// the arm this type already names — it does not invent a second admission concept. A `Declined*`
// arm here is a fact about why a site's identity is excluded from the executed roster; it is
// never itself the mechanism deciding execution order or eligibility for a DIFFERENT gate.
//
// `DeclinedLiveTree` IS DELETED HERE AND IN THE HOST REALIZATION AT THE ROOT. What it was: a
// site whose FILE declared `data live_tree_disposition: LiveTreeDisposition =
// ReadsLiveTree` was declined, on the premise that reaching the live tree implies "cannot run
// in the hermetic frame this floor runs". 778 identities across 112 files sat in that arm, and
// since the floor cut left `claim_executor --required-floor` as the only witness-executing
// consumer in the tree, they had no executing consumer at all — discovered every run, never
// run, for as long as the arm existed.
//
// WHY THE PREMISE WAS STALE: hermetic mode carries the checkout-input carve-out
// (`v1_interpreter` `hermetic_checkout_input_disposition_under`), under which a readonly
// `Filesystem.Read` or `Filesystem.List` of a path proven under the checkout root is INPUT access — the commit is
// the run's deterministic input — and not a host effect. That carve-out landed AFTER
// `live_tree_disposition` was authored. From that day "reads the live tree" no longer implied
// "cannot run hermetically", and the decline was a prediction standing where the interpreter
// already decides the same question exactly, per identity, at the effect boundary.
//
// WHAT REPLACES IT: nothing here. Every non-long, non-fixture identity is routed and executed,
// its route carried by the claim that runs it
// (`RequiredFloorClaim.execution_mode` — one route per identity, not a second vocabulary
// restating it), and the interpreter's own typed `HermeticHostEffectRefused` classifies the
// residue that genuinely has no hermetic arm. That residue reaches the receipt as
// `RequiredFloorOutcome.route_gap`, which BLOCKS: a claim that never reached its subject is
// not green, and it is not a failure either — the two have different remedies and are
// reported apart. THAT MECHANISM IS ALREADY LIVE IN THIS CHANGE, which is what makes the
// deletion safe to make next: the route-gap arm, its two-way roster join, and the
// `FloorDispositionJoinInexact` wall all execute here, over the 101 identities that reached it
// without this arm being touched at all.
//
// DELETION RECEIPT: run 32345970386 measured 626/783 passing under execution, and the staged
// blockers were closed before this root deletion. The live-tree disposition remains:
// it still feeds affected-set eligibility even though it no longer gates
// floor admission.
//
// A NOTE ON WHAT DID *NOT* MOVE: `live_tree_disposition` itself is not deleted and is not
// deprecated. It remains the declaration `reads_live_tree_effective` reads for AFFECTED-SET
// SELECTION ELIGIBILITY, which is a different question from "can this execute" and keeps its
// own single authority. Only the floor's forked, syntactic second copy was deleted.
// TWO ARMS SPEAK FOR IDENTITIES THE PROJECTION NEVER USED TO SEE AT ALL, and they are declines
// by the same authority rather than a second status vocabulary beside it.
//
// A disposition row used to exist only for a site the PREPARED SUBJECT offered. Preparation
// itself removes two populations before that point — a module whose path or name carries an
// exclusion substring, and (since the 2026-08-29 gate cut) every module the required gate's
// import closure does not reach — and a witness declared in one of those was neither planned nor
// declined. It left the floor's universe one level above the partition, which is the same
// silence `docs/plans/witness-execution-closure.md` records for the deleted live-tree decline:
// a population discovered by nothing, so counted by nothing.
//
// `DeclinedOutsideGateClosure` IS NOT `DeclinedOutsideRequiredGate`, and the distinction is
// load-bearing rather than descriptive: the second speaks for a module that WAS prepared and
// whose authored name missed every gate prefix, and the first for a module that was never
// prepared, resolved or offered. They are removed by different mechanisms, they are restored by
// different triggers, and after the gate cut they differ in population by two orders of
// magnitude — the closure arm is the population the §4b rung drop "Required gate reduced to the
// compiler floor" names. One label over both would report that drop's whole subject as a
// rounding error on a nearby count.
type RequiredFloorDisposition =
    Planned
  | PlannedAsChangedWitness
  | DeclinedLongModule { matched_prefix: String }
  | DeclinedFixtureMember { matched_prefix: String }
  | DeclinedOutsideRequiredGate
  | DeclinedOutsideGateClosure
  | DeclinedDiscoveryExcluded { matched_substring: String }
  | DeclinedCostDebt

// The changed-witness sublane is a second selector over the SAME identity-grain disposition,
// not a widening of `required_gate_prefixes` and not a terminal row beside a decline. Selection
// therefore replaces the static gate's answer with one planned arm before execution; every
// identity still has exactly one disposition and the ordinary answer is preserved unchanged for
// the standing population.
fn required_floor_disposition_with_changed_selection(
  ordinary: RequiredFloorDisposition,
  selected_as_changed_witness: Bool,
) -> RequiredFloorDisposition {
  if selected_as_changed_witness {
    PlannedAsChangedWitness {}
  } else {
    ordinary
  }
}

// ROSTER GROWTH IS BUDGET-BOUND AT THIS ADMISSION AUTHORITY.
//
// The required witnesses lane has no measured memory margin on the runner shape it consumes:
// main reaches the runner's memory limiter, and gunbc#8982's unchanged-head rerun was cancelled
// under sustained reclaim pressure after admission added a selected population. This is not a
// correctness veto on those identities and it is not a numeric roster ceiling. A new identity
// needs a named payment before admission grows; whether the payment is runner capacity, bounded
// result retention, or staged growth is deliberately left undecided here.
//
// THE ATTRIBUTION IS CONFOUNDED, AND THE TYPE KEEPS IT THAT WAY. The observed change both grew
// the roster and admitted the former declined-live population. That population was selected
// because it reads the live tree and is not established to have the retention shape of an
// average already-routed row. The receipt therefore cannot answer whether count alone reaches
// the limiter or whether the selected population retains disproportionately. A proposal may not
// turn either hypothesis into policy without a new instrument that separates them.
//
// THE INSTRUMENT IS NAMED, NOT TRANSCRIBED. Re-run the pull-request witnesses lane named below
// and read its cgroup heartbeat and terminal ledger. No RSS value, pressure counter, partial
// outcome count, or row ceiling is copied into this carrier. In particular, a cancelled fold's
// visible outcomes are lower bounds from a truncated fold, never a terminal ledger.
type RequiredFloorGrowthPayment =
    MoreRunnerMemory
  | BoundedTerminalResultRetention
  | GrowthStagedAgainstMeasuredHeadroom

type RequiredFloorGrowthAttribution =
  CountAndSelectedLiveTreePopulationConfounded {}

type RequiredFloorGrowthInstrument = RequiredCiWitnessesLane {
  workflow_run: String,
  invocation: String
}

type RequiredFloorLimiterStanding =
    NoMeasuredMarginAtRunnerLimiter
  | RequiredFloorMarginMeasured { instrument: String }

type RequiredFloorGrowthBudgetStanding = RequiredFloorGrowthBudgetBound {
  limiter: RequiredFloorLimiterStanding,
  attribution: RequiredFloorGrowthAttribution,
  candidate_payments: List<RequiredFloorGrowthPayment>,
  instrument: RequiredFloorGrowthInstrument,
  dissolution: DissolutionCondition
}

data required_floor_growth_budget_standing: RequiredFloorGrowthBudgetStanding = RequiredFloorGrowthBudgetBound {
    limiter: NoMeasuredMarginAtRunnerLimiter {},
    attribution: CountAndSelectedLiveTreePopulationConfounded {},
    candidate_payments: Cons {
      head: MoreRunnerMemory {},
      tail: Cons {
        head: BoundedTerminalResultRetention {},
        tail: Cons {
          head: GrowthStagedAgainstMeasuredHeadroom {},
          tail: Empty {}
        }
      }
    },
    instrument: RequiredCiWitnessesLane {
      workflow_run: "gunb-ai/gunbc actions run 33026738418",
      invocation: "claim_executor --required-ci --source-root dag --source-root src/v2 --required-lane witnesses"
    },
    dissolution: unbound_dissolution(description: "a dated required-lane receipt demonstrates margin for the admitted population, or admission structurally consumes a declared payment before roster growth")
}

// LONG-HOME STORAGE HYGIENE — A DIAGNOSTIC, NEVER AN ADMISSION INPUT.
//
// Two independent boolean facts about one file: does its PATH sit under a directory this repo
// treats as the long home (`/test/claim/long/`-shaped), and does its AUTHORED MODULE NAME start
// with one of `long_home_prefixes()` above (the fact `RequiredFloorDisposition` actually reads).
// The 2026-08-04 admission ruling already established that only the second fact may gate
// execution — see this module's header comment on `is_long_home`. `LongHomeStorageAgreement` is
// the derived cross-check that reports when the two facts diverge (a file physically homed in
// the long directory whose module name never opted in, or the reverse), so the divergence is
// visible without ever feeding back into admission. Naming it as a Cartesian product of two
// booleans rather than a bespoke four-arm guess is the point: the four arms below are nothing
// but `(path_is_long, module_is_long)`, and `long_home_storage_agreement` is total over that
// product, not a hand-picked subset.
type LongHomeStorageAgreement =
    LongPathAndLongModule
  | ShortPathAndShortModule
  | LongPathButExecutingModule
  | ShortPathButDeclinedModule

fn long_home_storage_agreement(path_is_long: Bool, module_is_long: Bool) -> LongHomeStorageAgreement {
  match path_is_long {
    True => match module_is_long {
      True => LongPathAndLongModule {}
      False => LongPathButExecutingModule {}
    }
    False => match module_is_long {
      True => ShortPathButDeclinedModule {}
      False => ShortPathAndShortModule {}
    }
  }
}

// THE ROSTER IS IMPORTED HERE SO IT IS REACHABLE, NOT SO IT IS RE-EXPORTED.
//
// The host evaluates the expected-red roster in the SAME frame it evaluates this manifest in,
// and that frame's scope is this module's closure. Without this edge the roster module is
// simply not in the world the frame was built from, and the call fails with `no such function`
// — which is what happened on the first run that reached admission-decode.
//
// The import names the roster's own function and nothing wraps it: the host still calls
// `v2.workflow.floor_expected_red.floor_expected_red_roster` by its real qualified name. A
// thin accessor here would be a second name for one authority, and this module would then own
// a fact it does not produce.
//
// `v2.workflow.floor_route_gap.floor_route_gap_roster` is imported for exactly the same reason
// and on exactly the same terms: the host evaluates it in this module's frame, so without the
// edge the module is not in the world that frame was built from and the call fails with `no
// such function` — the failure this comment was written to record the first time. The import is
// reachability, not re-export.
//
// `v2.workflow.floor_non_verdict.floor_non_verdict_roster` is the THIRD, and it arrived by
// making the same mistake a third time — with these two paragraphs already sitting here saying
// exactly what would happen. CI refused with `no declaration named
// 'v2.workflow.floor_non_verdict.floor_non_verdict_roster' in this execution's loaded index`,
// which is the same diagnostic, in the same place, for the same missing edge.
//
// THAT IS WORTH RECORDING AS A DEFECT IN THE MECHANISM RATHER THAN IN THREE AUTHORS. A prose
// note is not a wall: this list is a hand-maintained reachability closure that nothing derives
// and nothing checks, so every new roster the host evaluates in this frame must be remembered
// here by whoever adds it, and the failure surfaces only from a full CI run. The refusal is at
// least loud and located — the roster read fails closed, so the floor stops rather than
// proceeding with an empty roster — and that polarity is what keeps this a build failure
// instead of a silently permissive one. The structural repair is for the host's roster
// evaluations to derive their own reachability from the qualified names they call, at which
// point this import list and these paragraphs delete together.
//
// `v2.workflow.floor_cost_debt.floor_cost_debt_roster` is the FOURTH, and it arrived by making
// the same mistake a fourth time, with the paragraph directly above already naming it as a
// mechanism defect and predicting exactly this. The prediction was exact, including the cost:
// "the failure surfaces only from a full CI run". It cost a ~30-minute fold to learn, on a
// change whose whole subject is the cost of folds.
//
// FOUR OCCURRENCES IS NOT FOUR CARELESS AUTHORS. Each of us read a file that says, in prose, at
// the exact place we were editing, that this edge must be added by hand -- and the prose did not
// stop any of us, because a reader adding a roster is thinking about the roster's CONTENT while
// this edge is a fact about the FRAME the host will evaluate it in. The two live at different
// grains, which is precisely why a note cannot hold the invariant and the derivation named above
// can. Recorded as the fourth data point for that repair, not as a fourth apology.

//
// THREE PREFIXES, NOT ONE, and the count is what found that. A single `test.claim.long.` test
// excluded 42 rows of an expected ~310, because the long home is not one namespace: 64 modules
// there declare `v2.test.long.`, 10 declare `test.claim.long.`, and one declares
// `v2.test.claim.long.`. The directory looks uniform and the authored names are not.
//
// One file in that directory declares no long namespace at all —
// `qualified_declaration_reference_emit_cross_module_witness_test.dag` declares
// `test.claim.qualified_declaration_reference_emit_cross_module_witness` — so it stays in the
// floor. That is the honest consequence of reading the declaration instead of the path, and it
// is the right consequence: the file sits in a directory claiming one thing while its module
// says another, and only one of those is a fact the program can read.
//
// The one thing worth keeping from the version this replaces: the test is on the module's
// AUTHORED NAME, not on the file path. `is_long_home` in the host tested whether a path
// contained `/test/claim/long/`, which made a directory the admission authority — the exact
// root cause the 2026-08-04 ruling names — and decided at file grain, so one expensive witness
// took every cheap sibling in its file out of the floor with it. Every witness in the long home
// already declares `module test.claim.long.<name>` on its own first line, so the fact was
// authored all along and the host was reading a shadow of it.
//
// That is the whole change. No grant table, no disposition lattice, no second mechanism: one
// prefix test over the roster below, applied once by the host that already holds every witness's
// authored module name.
//
// `WitnessDisposition` — a five-arm coproduct with no consumer, whose one predicate
// `disposition_is_required_floor` was never called — was deleted with the manifest fold rather
// than left standing as a model of a decision nothing made. Wiring wet lanes and cadences is a
// real modelling question, and when it is asked it will be asked against a live consumer.
//
// These rows are NOT COVERED by anything. The host reports the count every run.
fn long_home_prefixes() -> List<String> {
  Cons {
    head: "test.claim.long.",
    tail: Cons {
      head: "v2.test.long.",
      tail: Cons {
        head: "v2.test.claim.long.",
        tail: Empty {}
      }
    }
  }
}

// THE THIRD DECLINE: A FIXTURE MEMBER IS NOT A WITNESS, AND ENROLLING ONE IS NOT DEBT.
//
// A `test fn` under `v2.test.fixture.walk_plan_stage.` is not a claim about the compiler. It is
// an INPUT to another claim: `claim_executor --plan-entry
// src/v2/test/fixture/walk_plan_stage/plan.dag --plan-function <recipe>` drives it as a
// `RunnableSingleClaim` row authored in that fixture's own `common.dag`, and the recipes in
// `plan.dag` are what assert on the result. The member is the specimen; the recipe is the
// witness.
//
// WHY THE FOLD MUST NOT RUN THEM, and this is a correctness argument rather than a cost one.
// Two of the nine are red BY CONSTRUCTION and can never green: `failure_red_member`'s body is
// the literal `false`, and `recursion_refusal_member` calls itself so the interpreter's
// call-depth refusal fires — that refusal IS what the recursion-refusal recipe observes. The
// other seven perform the host effects the recipes are about, and `stage2_marker` is the sharp
// one: it writes `target/walk_plan_stage_stage2_ran`, the exact path
// `walk_plan_stage_failure_barrier_recipe` asserts "must stay absent" when stage 1 reds. Giving
// that member a hermetic route would make the floor write the marker that another fixture's
// oracle is defined by — a run corrupting the input of the test it is running.
//
// WHAT THIS COST WHILE IT WAS UNSTATED. All nine were discovered, planned and enrolled: two on
// `v2.workflow.floor_expected_red` and seven on `v2.workflow.floor_route_gap`. Both rosters
// declare themselves temporary, monotone and self-emptying, and neither can ever shed these
// nine — the first two by construction, the seven because the honest route for them is no route
// at all. Nine permanent rows on two shrinking ledgers is the skip list both headers are
// carefully not, arrived at without anyone choosing it. The fixture's own `common.dag` note has
// asserted the correct state of the world all along — "discovery-excluded at dir grain … and
// declares no enrolled witness rows" — and that sentence stopped being true at the floor cut,
// which is when a directory stopped being what discovery reads.
//
// THE MECHANISM IS THE ONE ALREADY SANCTIONED, deliberately and not by analogy: the test is on
// the module's AUTHORED NAME, exactly as `long_home_prefixes` is, for the reason the 2026-08-04
// ruling gives — a directory must not decide an admission question. `v2.test.fixture.` is NOT
// the prefix, and the narrower one is not caution: 27 modules declare `v2.test.fixture.` and
// only the walk_plan_stage family authors `test fn`s, so the broad prefix would pre-admit a
// decline for 26 modules whose behaviour nobody has examined. A prefix earns its rows.
//
// THIS IS NOT A SELECTION SHRINK, which is the objection it will draw. The 2026-08-13 directive
// forbids SELECTION from shrinking the roster; a home policy answers a different question, and
// the difference here is that these nine LOSE NO COVERAGE — they keep the executing consumer
// they have always had, the recipes, which is more than a route-gap row can say. Declining a
// site whose only consumer is the fold deletes coverage; declining one the fold was never the
// consumer of returns it to its owner.
//
// DISSOLVES when the fixture stops authoring `test fn`s — if `claim_executor` grows a claim form
// that does not require one, this roster empties and the arm goes with it. There is no
// empty-roster refusal here, for the same reason `long_home_prefixes` has none.
fn fixture_home_prefixes() -> List<String> {
  Cons {
    head: "v2.test.fixture.walk_plan_stage.",
    tail: Empty {}
  }
}

// THE REQUIRED GATE: A STATIC ROSTER, BY OPERATOR RULING (2026-08-29, THE CI BANKRUPTCY).
//
// On the last green run before this row landed the required floor folded 11,996 witnesses
// through a whole-corpus strict preparation: 27 minutes to prepare 4,171 modules on one core,
// 32 more to fold, 87 wall minutes for the workflow, and ~50 squash-merges a day validated
// against a main that had moved dozens of commits by merge time -- fifteen consecutive red
// runs on main from two integration collisions no PR could have seen. The operator's ruling:
// the required gate is the compiler floor and nothing else; the rest of the corpus runs as
// receipts, not as merge conditions.
//
// THIS ROSTER IS THAT RULING AS A MECHANISM. A site whose authored module name carries none
// of these prefixes is `DeclinedOutsideRequiredGate` -- counted, written to the disposition
// TSV, reported in the run's announcement, never silently skipped. It is decided AFTER cost
// debt, so a rostered cost-debt identity outside the gate still enters the seen set and the
// roster's staleness check keeps its meaning -- and the host prepares ONLY
// the import closure of the modules these prefixes name (`v1_compiler.cli_run`
// `assemble_prepared_subject_closure`), so the cost is denominated in the gate, not the corpus.
//
// WHAT IS IN. `v2.test.` is the whole v2 compiler suite. The `test.claim.` rows are the v1
// compiler-floor witnesses -- inference, occurrence binding, declared-type inhabitance,
// coproduct soundness, namespace resolution, parse, the self-host behavioral witnesses, and
// XL-0's repair-input split plus namespace control-plane standing -- and the census that tests
// this roster's own arms. Nothing here is a product, fleet, deploy,
// secret, dashboard or roadmap witness; those are exactly what the ruling moved off the gate.
//
// WHAT THIS IS NOT. Not a selection shrink (the 2026-08-13 directive): a selection derives the
// population from the change; this roster is a constant the operator signed, and the derived
// selection the CI-cost lane names ("decide what matters before paying to prepare everything")
// replaces the constant when it lands. It is a declared rung drop --
// `gunbc.rung_drop` `required_gate_bankruptcy` -- with that as its restoration trigger.
//
// A ROW IS ADDED HERE AND NOWHERE ELSE, and adding one is a wall-clock decision: the floor's
// per-claim cost receipt names what each row costs.
fn required_gate_prefixes() -> List<String> {
  [
    "v2.test.",
    "test.claim.infer_",
    "test.claim.occurrence_binding_",
    "test.claim.declared_type_",
    "test.claim.coproduct_payload_soundness_witness_test",
    "test.claim.match_exhaustiveness_coproduct_witness",
    "test.claim.namespace_",
    "test.claim.self_host_",
    "test.claim.type_reference_",
    "test.claim.import_admission_closure_membership_witness_test",
    "test.claim.import_shadowed_by_local_definition_witness_test",
    "test.claim.class_b_import_closure_binding_refusal_witness",
    "test.claim.floor_resolve_realization_witness",
    "test.claim.resolved_call_emission_identity_witness_test",
    "test.claim.dag_parse_continuation_operator_witness_test",
    "test.claim.parse_test",
    "test.claim.v1_dag_parse_witness",
    "test.claim.generic_lambda_parameter_binding_witness",
    "test.claim.arity_zero_import_refusal_witness",
    "test.claim.bare_name_",
    "test.claim.legacy_binding_",
    "test.claim.compiler_frontend_program_status_witness",
    "test.claim.discovery_census_witness_test",
    "test.claim.host_budget_source_witness",
    "test.claim.typed_module_cache_capacity_witness",
    "test.claim.whole_corpus_compile_admission_witness",
    "test.claim.memory_stall_refusal_witness",
  ]
}

// test.claim.memory_stall_refusal_witness JOINS THE SAME FAMILY BY THE SAME TEST: it judges
// the arm that stops a resolve which is thrashing instead of computing (gunbc.
// memory_stall_refusal), which is the state the three modules below cannot see -- budget
// readable and admitted, cap derived and enforced, and the machine still unable to deliver
// the budget beside its own page cache. The failure it closes is the 2026-08-30 default-VM
// treadmill: four whole-closure runs holding 2-4.5 hours with no diagnostic and no exit.
// Same exact-module-name rule as below, for the same reason.
//
// THE LAST THREE ROWS BEFORE IT, ADDED 2026-08-30, AND WHY THEY BELONG UNDER THE RULING ABOVE
// RATHER THAN BESIDE IT.
//
// They are the host memory budget's three witness modules, and each one is a COMPILER FLOOR
// witness by the ruling's own test -- none is a product, fleet, deploy, secret, dashboard or
// roadmap witness. What they judge is whether a compile may BEGIN and what bounds the memory
// used to RESOLVE the corpus: gunbc.whole_corpus_compile_admission decides admission before
// anything is indexed, gunbc.typed_module_cache_capacity derives the eviction bound that runs
// WHILE modules are being resolved, and gunbc.host_budget_source owns the source those two
// admit against. A compiler that cannot bound its own resolution is below the floor, not above
// it -- and the failure this closed was a rc=137 SIGKILL of the corpus regen with no
// diagnostic at all.
//
// WHY THEY ARE EXACT MODULE NAMES AND NOT `test.claim.host_`. That prefix would sweep in 28
// modules under dag/test/claim/host/ -- converge, deploy-apply, disk-reclaim, standup -- which
// perform real host effects and are precisely the population the 2026-08-29 ruling moved OFF
// the gate. A prefix that admits the subject by admitting its neighbours is not the same
// decision, and this roster's cost is a wall-clock one.
//
// WHAT THEY COST, MEASURED RATHER THAN ESTIMATED. All 30 functions across the three modules
// were executed on one runner via claim_batch: 17/17, 8/8 and 5/5 PASS, every one between 0
// and 15 ms wall, well inside the per-claim cost line. Adding them is not free, and it is the
// smallest non-zero price at which this subject's evidence executes at all.
//
// WHY NOT LEAVE THEM OUT. Because they were already out, and that is the defect. All three
// modules sat OUTSIDE this roster while carrying the walls for a live fail-open, so their
// witnesses were `DeclinedOutsideGateClosure` on every run -- specification without execution,
// the trap DESIGN section 5 names, in the evidence for a class that had already killed a
// production run twice. v2.workflow.floor_changed_witness is what surfaced it: it projected
// this change's own 14 changed identities as `declined ... not_executed` and refused the
// floor. The roster row is the answer that gate is asking for.

// THE ADMISSION ORDER, HELD HERE RATHER THAN IN WHOEVER WALKS THE CORPUS.
//
// `RequiredFloorDisposition` above names the three arms. The ordering is long home, then fixture
// home, then planned. This decision previously lived only in the host loop that walks the
// discovered files
// (`v1_compiler.cli_run` `run_required_floor`). That made the type an authority over the
// vocabulary and not over the decision, and a second walker (the .dag census this function was
// added for) would have had to re-author the order and could have re-authored it differently.
//
// THE ORDER IS THE ONE THE HOST REALIZES AND IT IS LOAD-BEARING, not alphabetical: a fixture
// member inside a long home reports the long-home storage disposition first; every other fixture
// member reports the ownership fact that its own recipe already executes it. Live-tree standing
// is absent from this API because the root cut makes it irrelevant to floor admission.
type ModulePrefixMatch =
    ModulePrefixUnmatched
  | ModulePrefixMatched { prefix: String }

fn first_module_prefix_match(module_path: String, prefixes: List<String>) -> ModulePrefixMatch {
  match prefixes {
    Empty {} => ModulePrefixUnmatched {}
    Cons { head: h, tail: t } =>
      if starts_with(s: module_path, prefix: h) {
        ModulePrefixMatched { prefix: h }
      } else {
        first_module_prefix_match(module_path: module_path, prefixes: t)
      }
  }
}

// THE THIRD DECLINE ARRIVED (2026-08-27), AND WITH IT THE COLLISION THE ORDER ABOVE WAS KEPT
// FOR. `DeclinedCostDebt` is claim-grain -- it is a membership test over
// `v2.workflow.floor_cost_debt` on a qualified identity -- while the two declines above are
// module-grain, so a single site CAN legitimately satisfy a cost-debt decline and a long-home or
// live-tree decline at once. That is exactly the subject the next-rung trigger above named as
// owed, and it is now authorable.
//
// COST DEBT IS ORDERED LAST, DELIBERATELY. A row that already declines for a home reason does
// not execute for that reason, so a cost-debt row over it withholds nothing that was going to
// run -- and reporting it as `DeclinedCostDebt` would tell a reader to make it cheaper when the
// correct remedy is to delete the roster line. Ordering it last means such a row keeps its home
// decline, never enters the cost-debt reverse join, and is reported as a STALE roster row by
// `v1_compiler.cli_run` -- which blocks, and whose diagnostic says to delete the line. The order
// is therefore load-bearing in a way the previous two-decline order never was: swapping it would
// silently convert a stale roster row into a permanent, plausible-looking withhold.
//
// THIS FUNCTION TAKES ONLY THE MODULE-GRAIN INPUTS AND STAYS THAT WAY. The cost-debt decision is
// applied by the caller, which holds the identity; folding a `qualified_identity` parameter in
// here would make every module-grain caller invent one. The ORDER is what this function owns and
// the order is what the caller must not re-author -- so the caller applies cost debt only to a
// site this function already answered `Planned` for, which is the same precedence expressed at
// the only grain each half can see.
// THE DISPOSITION TAKES THE QUALIFIED IDENTITY, NOT ONLY THE MODULE PATH, AND THE REASON IS THE
// FOURTH ARM. The two home declines are MODULE facts -- every `test fn` in a long or fixture home
// shares its module's disposition -- so a `module_path` parameter was sufficient while those were
// the only declines. The cost decline is not a module fact: it names ONE witness by its qualified
// identity, and a module may hold a withheld row beside a running one.
//
// AN EARLIER REVISION OF THIS CHANGE LEFT THIS FUNCTION AT `module_path` ALONE while the host
// declined by identity, and two Rust comments cited THIS function for a precedence it did not
// carry. That is the §3 violation in its exact form: the executing floor and the modeled
// `//:required` aggregate answering differently about one population, with `gunbc.discovery_census`
// classifying all 266 withheld rows as `Planned` while the fold declined them. Caught by
// review 57235; the divergence is closed by giving the model the input it was missing rather than
// by teaching the census to special-case the host.
//
// THE COST ARM BRANCHES WITH `if`, NOT WITH A VARIANT MATCH, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING RATHER THAN
// STYLE. `floor_cost_debt_holds` returns the corpus `Bool`, which is realized NATIVELY as a machine
// scalar; `v2.std.algebra` `any` itself matches it as lowercase `true`/`false`. The interpreter's
// `match_pattern` carries VariantPattern arms for Variant, Record, List, Str, Int and Null and NO
// arm for a native bool, so a `True`/`False` variant pattern over this value matches nothing and
// falls through at RUNTIME -- it parses, it typechecks, and it dies with
// `cause=pattern-match-failure`.
//
// AN EARLIER REVISION OF THIS FUNCTION MATCHED `True {}` / `False {}` AND ERRORED 11 OF 19
// WITNESSES in `discovery_census_witness_test`, several of which never call this function and went
// down through the shared census fold. The braces were NOT the defect: measured by still-cat-738
// through the real binary, the bare `True` spelling fails identically, and a record pattern against
// a genuine nullary variant (`A {}` over `type T = A | B`) matches perfectly well. So the class is
// not pattern arity -- it is a declaration offering arms no value can present, the same family as
// the interpreter's named StringRealizationStraddle. Branching with `if` sidesteps the straddle
// instead of depending on it.
//
// PRECEDENCE IS DECIDED HERE AND MIRRORED THERE. Cost debt is LAST, so a rostered identity that
// also declines for a home reason keeps its home decline. That is what makes such a roster line
// surface as STALE -- it never enters the cost-debt seen set -- instead of as a withhold
// indistinguishable from a real one.
fn required_floor_site_disposition(
  module_path: String,
  identity: String,
) -> RequiredFloorDisposition {
  match first_module_prefix_match(module_path: module_path, prefixes: long_home_prefixes()) {
    ModulePrefixMatched { prefix: p } => DeclinedLongModule { matched_prefix: p }
    ModulePrefixUnmatched =>
      match first_module_prefix_match(module_path: module_path, prefixes: fixture_home_prefixes()) {
        ModulePrefixMatched { prefix: p } => DeclinedFixtureMember { matched_prefix: p }
        ModulePrefixUnmatched =>
          if floor_cost_debt_holds(name: identity) {
            DeclinedCostDebt {}
          } else {
            match first_module_prefix_match(module_path: module_path, prefixes: required_gate_prefixes()) {
              ModulePrefixUnmatched => DeclinedOutsideRequiredGate {}
              ModulePrefixMatched { prefix: _ } => Planned {}
            }
          }
      }
  }
}

// NO `required_floor_disposition_is_planned` LIVES HERE. An earlier revision of this change
// carried one — a `Bool` over this closed coproduct — and it was the same mechanical shape the
// rest of this branch dissolved three of (review 55984). Its only consumer filtered a roster with
// it, keeping the routed side and discarding the declined one, which is the fact the census
// exists to carry. `gunbc.discovery_census` `census_partition` now descends over these arms in one
// pass and returns both sides, so a fifth arm must be classified rather than absorbed into
// `false`, and the roster cannot be produced without its exclusions.




// THE EVALUATION DEADLINE FOR ONE CLAIM IS WALL TIME, AND IT HAS TWO TIERS.
//
// Historical receipt (retired 2026-08-18): resolution_divergence_silent_pick_gate_keystone_holds
// checked whether parent-plan telemetry was captured only AFTER a second whole-corpus resolve —
// a precondition-ordering defect that cost 526s/+2.66GB every run while enrolled expected-red hid
// 43% of floor slow time. See gunbc.ci_layer_roots
// resolution_divergence_silent_pick_gate_retirement_receipt. Wall ceilings exist for I/O-heavy
// witnesses whose CPU stays under eval budget.
//
// TWO TIERS, because "too slow" and "unacceptable" are different verdicts with different
// remedies. The warning tier reports a row that is an order of magnitude above where an
// ordinary witness should land and lets it finish; the hard tier stops it. Collapsing them
// loses the population that is heading for the cliff but has not reached it -- and that
// population is the one worth fixing, because a witness at the hard ceiling has already cost
// the run its full budget by the time anyone hears about it.
//
// THE NUMBERS (operator, 2026-08-17): 500ms HARD, 100ms warn. 500 was previously the warning
// under a 5000ms ceiling; the ceiling moved down onto it because 5000ms is magnitudes above
// where any witness should end. The measured median is 1.0ms, so an ordinary row sits two
// orders of magnitude under the warning and three under the ceiling.
//
// A row over the ceiling has exactly two honest remedies, and RELOCATION IS NOT ONE OF THEM:
// reduce what the witness reaches for, or enroll it in a lane that declares its own dated
// ceiling AND names the row as an executing consumer.
//
// This comment used to offer "move it to a designated long/ home that CI does not run", which
// is the move the 2026-08-04 admission ruling forbids and which the interpreter's own refusal
// text argues against three files away (v1_interpreter.rs, EvaluationBudgetExceeded: "RELOCATING
// THE FILE DOES NOT DISCHARGE IT"). Two authorities in one tree disagreed about the remedy, and
// this was the one recommending the defect. Nothing executes a long/ home -- the tree carries
// witnesses.yml and fleet-converge.yml, and the falsifier cadence died in the floor cut -- so
// relocating a row deletes its coverage while retaining its source, which is the gunbc#7762
// specimen exactly. Found by quiet-ibex-39 while censusing the runaways.
// The warn value is a judgement -- the operator set the ceiling, not the warning -- and it is
// placed an order of magnitude below the ceiling so the signal arrives while a fix is still
// cheap.
//
// They are stated here rather than imported from the fast-lane rule because the fast lane is a
// PLACEMENT question -- which checks may ride the PR path -- and this is an EXECUTION question:
// how long a single claim may run before the frame refuses. They agree today, and reusing one
// for the other would make that coincidence load-bearing.
//
// SPLIT, operator ruling 2026-08-19 (BUDGET POLICY CUT), CORRECTED the same day by the
// operator's superseding message: the single number that stood here used to answer three
// questions at once -- when to interrupt CPU, when to interrupt wall, and what a completed
// claim is allowed to have cost -- and that fusion is exactly the defect the ruling names: a
// scheduler-sensitive wall crossing must not decide that a pure computation is too expensive,
// which is what pushed innocent rows into censorship under co-tenant load. THREE independent
// numbers now stand where one stood, and the superseding correction went further than the
// first split: CPU and wall are not one shared "safety limit" either -- see below.
//
// `required_floor_claim_cost_line_ms` is DIAGNOSTIC ONLY -- it decides NOTHING about admission,
// quarantine, or a cost-debt population. That is stronger than "it only classifies a completed
// claim": the operator's superseding ruling retires this figure from completed-cost admission
// too, not only from interruption. It was one 1527ms observation plus a scheduler quantum, and
// the exact 1792ms/1928ms pair observed since shows terminal measurements vary materially
// across admissible envelopes -- a single literal line cannot honestly gate a merge decision on
// that spread. The figure may remain as historical calibration and diagnostic comparison
// evidence only; nothing in the required-floor loop may read it to decide whether a run blocks
// (and nothing in the live loop does today -- `RequiredFloorClaim.cost_line_ms` has no
// admission-path consumer; the only reader is the diagnostic `exceeds_completed_cost_line`).
//
// `required_floor_claim_cpu_safety_limit_ms` and `required_floor_claim_wall_safety_limit_ms`
// are TWO INDEPENDENT SAFETY DEADLINES, never a scalar mechanically copied into both clocks.
// For a Hermetic pure in-process claim, CPU protects against runaway evaluation while wall
// protects against a blocked or descheduled process -- different jobs, so the wall limit is
// independently derived and LOOSER than the CPU limit, so ordinary host scheduling cannot
// preempt a computation that is still inside its CPU envelope. TEMPORARY restoration-envelope
// values, not re-founded permanent ceilings, and both are deliberately far above where any
// ordinary witness should land (measured per-witness median ~1ms) so neither should fire against
// a healthy claim.
//
// THE GUARANTEE, REWRITTEN RATHER THAN ANNOTATED (PREEMPTION-1, operator-directed, 2026-08-19;
// reworked on gunbc#8584 per royal-hawk-392's REWORK): these two limits are cooperatively
// observed while interpreted execution keeps reaching `eval_expr`'s own stride-poll checkpoints
// -- they are NOT hard bounds across an opaque host operation unless that operation separately
// carries a deadline-aware contract or a killable process boundary. Whichever deadline a poll
// DOES observe crossing raises a genuinely terminal-before-verdict occurrence
// (`ClaimSafetyOutcome.SafetyInterrupted`, below); a claim whose cost accrues where no poll can
// land instead completes and is classified `CompletedPastSafetyLimit`, which blocks identically
// (`claim_safety_outcome_blocks`) but is never mislabeled as an interrupt it did not receive. The
// typed model -- `ClaimPreemptionReachability`, `ClaimSafetyOutcome`, `claim_safety_outcome`,
// `claim_safety_outcome_blocks`, the migration wall, and the §4b rung drop -- is declared further
// down this file rather than described here in prose a second time; the measured receipt behind
// it (floor run 32301212975, `root_d_checkpoint_scalar_declared_arity_witness_holds` reaching a
// verdict at 60317ms CPU against this module's 5000ms `required_floor_claim_cpu_safety_limit_ms`,
// twelve times over and uninterrupted, not a first occurrence -- 2792-2855ms isolated per
// docs/plans/witness-cost-first-touch-attribution.md) is what that model derives
// `CompletedPastSafetyLimit { preemption: OpaqueHostCallUnbounded { operation:
// "compile_dag_rust_emit_check" } }` FROM, not a claim this comment makes independently of it.
//
// A completed claim's cost is judged on exactly one clock, chosen by the witness's PURPOSE and
// never by measurement -- `v2.workflow.required_floor`'s claims execute Hermetic (pure in-process
// evaluation), so CPU is the basis; a lane that later admits an execution mode whose purpose is
// external or blocking interaction picks wall instead, but never lets the clock that happened to
// read higher decide. (This clock-selection sentence is orthogonal to admission — see the
// diagnostic-only note above; `required_floor_cost_basis` selects a clock for a number that no
// longer gates anything.)
// ENFORCEMENT GRAIN (2026-08-18): cooperative deadlines compare whole milliseconds on
// shared CI hosts under floor load. The 2026-08-18 witnesses fold measured a 1527ms ceiling
// (test.claim.transport_script_wall_compile_red.wall_red_string_join_into_retained_record_refuses)
// under whole-corpus pressure while the per-witness median remains ~1ms — 1552ms is
// measured-max plus one scheduler-quantum, retained as calibration evidence only.
// RE-POINTED TO 100ms WITH THE CEILING RESTORATION (2026-08-27), because at 1552 it became
// UNFIREABLE and therefore a decoration. Under a 500ms CPU ceiling no claim can complete above
// 1552ms -- the safety limit stops it first -- so the comparison would have been permanently
// green BY CONSTRUCTION while still being reported and counted, which DESIGN section 4b calls
// worse than absent because it is cited as coverage. The figure keeps the value the operator
// paired with 500 in the 2026-08-17 ruling.
//
// IT STILL DECIDES NOTHING, and that is unchanged by the re-point: it has no admission-path
// consumer, and the only reader remains the diagnostic `exceeds_completed_cost_line`. What it
// now does again is name the population between an ordinary row (~1ms median) and the ceiling --
// the rows heading for the cliff that have not reached it, which is the population worth fixing
// before it blocks anyone.
fn required_floor_claim_cost_line_ms() -> Int {
  100
}

// THE CPU SAFETY DEADLINE: 500ms (operator ruling 2026-08-17, RESTORED 2026-08-27).
//
// THIS IS NOT A NEW NUMBER. It is the ruling that has stood since gunbc#8283 and was never
// repealed. It is written here with its own provenance because the two raises that displaced it
// each cited an authority that did not cover the value, and a reader who finds only the raises
// will re-derive them as legitimate:
//
//   500  -> 1552  (gunbc#8514, 2026-08-19 01:26). NO OPERATOR RULING. The whole justification
//                  was "the 2026-08-18 witnesses fold measured a 1527ms ceiling ... 1552ms is
//                  measured-max plus one scheduler-quantum". That is a merge-blocking literal
//                  grounded in a measurement of the same current tree -- the oracle DESIGN
//                  section 5 forbids by name. The ceiling was set to whatever the slowest row
//                  happened to cost, which is a change detector, not a budget. Its own comment
//                  said "not an open slack ratchet"; it ratcheted 3.2x eleven hours later.
//   1552 -> 5000  (gunbc#8533, 2026-08-19 12:24). Cited "operator ruling 2026-08-19, superseding
//                  correction" on both clocks. That ruling was about the SPLIT -- that CPU and
//                  wall are two independent deadlines rather than one scalar copied into both.
//                  The operator's own quoted words in that diff, "DO NOT set CPU and wall to the
//                  same 5000ms", PRESUPPOSE 5000 was already on the table: it is a ruling about
//                  the mechanical copy, not about the magnitude. The value rode in as a
//                  passenger on a ruling about something else, which is the AUTHORITY
//                  SUBSTITUTION failure mode in DESIGN's recurring list -- both halves true,
//                  only the arrow invented.
//
// Neither raise named a restoration trigger or a date, so "TEMPORARY" and "restoration
// envelope" described nothing that could end. The population that had accumulated under the
// 10x drift is frozen in `v2.workflow.floor_cost_debt` as a monotone debt contract and withheld
// from execution; it is not granted a higher ceiling, because a row that broke the budget does
// not get an exemption to keep spending it.
//
// This is the TIGHTER of the two limits. It protects a pure in-process claim against runaway
// evaluation on the CPU clock; the wall limit below is independently derived and looser.
fn required_floor_claim_cpu_safety_limit_ms() -> Int {
  500
}

// THE WALL SAFETY DEADLINE: 8000ms. Independently derived and deliberately LOOSER, and the
// operator ruling that stands over it is still the 2026-08-19 one -- CPU and wall are never one
// scalar copied into both clocks ("DO NOT set CPU and wall to the same 5000ms").
//
// THIS FIGURE WAS 2000ms FOR ONE CI RUN AND THAT WAS WRONG, recorded here because the mistake is
// instructive and the corrected derivation is unreadable without it. 2000 was derived correctly
// by method -- the worst wall observed among rows INSIDE the CPU budget, times a headroom factor
// -- and from the wrong population: it was measured before the roster was rebased onto marginal
// cost, on data where a first-touch fill payer carried a large CPU figure and was therefore
// EXCLUDED from the "inside the CPU budget" population the derivation reads.
//
// WHAT THE REBASE EXPOSED, and it is an asymmetry in the measurement rather than in this
// constant: `v1_compiler.cli_run` `run_claim_measured` subtracts shared-artifact fill from CPU
// and DOES NOT subtract it from WALL. So a fill payer's marginal CPU goes to ~0 while its wall
// keeps the whole shared build. Those rows then land inside the CPU budget and dominate the wall
// population, which is exactly where the derivation reads. Measured on run 33136345225:
// `test.claim.alias_identity_across_unwrap_witness`
// `a_second_alias_still_emits_its_own_name_and_not_the_first` reports
// `marginal_cpu_ms=0 fill_cpu_ms=3772` with `wall_ms=3782` -- the CPU clock exonerates it and a
// 2000ms wall clock condemns it, for the same work, in the same run. Sixteen rows were in that
// state.
//
// 8000ms TIGHTENS THIS LIMIT. Main carries 10000ms; this figure is a REDUCTION, and the two
// figures beside it are reductions too (CPU 5000 -> 500, completed-cost line 1552 -> 100). An
// earlier revision of this block framed 8000 as a loosening that needed a runway, on a reading
// that it had been raised to accommodate rows a defect was inflating. It had not. The framing
// was inverted and is deleted rather than annotated, because a constant described as a
// concession ranks for removal while the same constant described as a tightening does not.
//
// THE DERIVATION IS STILL BLIND, INDEPENDENTLY OF THAT, and this half survives the correction
// because it is a fact about the METHOD rather than about the direction of the change. The
// method was: take the worst wall observed among rows inside the CPU budget, times headroom. A
// first-touch payer's marginal CPU is netted to ~0 while its wall carries the whole shared
// build, so the rows that dominate the wall population are precisely the rows whose wall figure
// is not about them. The general form is worth more than this constant: A DERIVATION WHOSE
// INPUT IS FILTERED BY THE QUANTITY BEING DERIVED CANNOT SEE ITS OWN WORST CASE. It will look
// well-grounded, because the sample is real.
//
// WALL IS NOT NETTED OF FILL, AND MUST NOT BE. Netting was proposed and is REFUSED here, on the
// carrier that owns the question. Two independent reasons, and the second is the load-bearing
// one:
//
//   UNITS. The existing accumulator records `v1_interpreter::thread_cpu_nanos`. Subtracting it
//   from a wall figure under-subtracts, because wall fill >= CPU fill always and strictly more
//   under contention -- visible in this file's own specimen, cpu fill 3728 against wall 3782 --
//   and it breaks the invariant that makes the split a measurement rather than a discount, since
//   `marginal + fill = measured` holds on CPU by construction and would be false on wall.
//
//   SUBJECT. The wall deadline's job, declared above, is catching a claim that is genuinely
//   BLOCKED OR STUCK. Cost accruing inside the shared build reaches no stride-poll checkpoint --
//   that is exactly the region this file already carries a §4b rung drop for
//   (`OpaqueHostCallUnbounded`) -- so the raw wall reading is the ONLY detector that region has.
//   Netting it would delete the sole instrument over the sole blind spot, and would do it
//   wearing an accounting label rather than announcing itself as a safety regression.
//
// SO THE 15 PAYERS ARE NOT A NETTING PROBLEM. Measured on run 33136345225, every row whose fill
// alone exceeds 2000ms carries marginal CPU of 0-2ms, and NO row in that population has marginal
// CPU above 500ms -- 15 of 15 are pure borrowed cost, zero are genuine own-work. They failed an
// earlier 2000ms figure on this branch, which is abandoned; against 8000ms they are inside, and
// the raw deadline still catches the case it exists for (the specimen it was written against
// burned 504 SECONDS of wall while its CPU never approached its limit).
//
// DISSOLUTION TRIGGER, and it is NOT a wall accumulator. `gunbc.census_memo_seed_growth` rules
// that the admissible repair is to STOP RECOMPUTING, never to raise the line, and that argument
// is clock-agnostic. The occupancy these 15 rows exhibit is a shared build being recomputed, not
// a claim being slow; when that recomputation is eliminated the occupancy disappears from wall
// and CPU together, no netting is required anywhere, and this figure is re-derived against a
// population that no longer contains a first-touch payer at all. The trigger is therefore the
// elimination of the recomputation, not an instrument for attributing it more precisely --
// attributing borrowed cost better still leaves the process occupied.
fn required_floor_claim_wall_safety_limit_ms() -> Int {
  8000
}

// THE WARN TIER IS DELETED (2026-08-20). It is not lowered, not re-derived from the envelope,
// and not retained as a nonblocking console diagnostic.
//
// A first attempt on this branch DID derive it (a fifth of the CPU safety limit) on the reading
// that the defect was the number. That reading was wrong and is recorded here rather than
// quietly replaced: the defect was the TIER. A warning is not a terminal disposition — a claim
// either reaches a verdict within its requirement or it refuses, and DESIGN §5 rules that a
// failure arm must refuse rather than widen. "Over a threshold, reported, allowed to finish" is
// neither arm; it is an observation with no remedy attached, and deriving a better threshold for
// it would have kept a second quasi-limit in the console beside the two that actually decide
// anything. Re-pinning and deriving are both answers to the wrong question.
//
// WHAT IT COST WHILE IT STOOD: the value was a bare `100` while the safety envelope moved to
// 5000ms CPU / 10000ms wall, so it fired on the MEDIAN witness — several hundred `[floor-witness-slow]`
// lines per run, each reporting that an ordinary row was ordinary (measured on run 32324402687's
// console, where ordinary rows land at 100-600ms). A signal whose frequency is one by
// construction carries no information, and it buried the terminal lines a reader is there for.
//
// WHAT IT NEVER BOUGHT: `RequiredFloorOutcome.over_warn` was incremented on every crossing and
// READ BY NOTHING — no gate, no summary, no artifact. The tier's whole output was console volume.
//
// The two safety deadlines survive unchanged and remain the only claim-level limits; the
// completed-cost line remains diagnostic-only. The tier-ordering refusal that stood beside them
// (`ClaimBudgetTiersInverted`) is deleted with the tier: it existed solely to keep the warn below
// the tighter deadline, and CPU and wall are independent axes rather than ordered tiers, so with
// the warn gone there is no ordering left to violate.

// PREEMPTION-1 TYPED MODEL (operator-directed, 2026-08-19; reworked on gunbc#8584 per
// royal-hawk-392's REWORK, which correctly held that a prose note is not the model S1 asked
// for -- everything above this point that reads as a description of the residue is now backed
// by the carriers below, with a real derivation and a real consumer, not a comment restating
// itself as done). THE GUARANTEE TEXT ITSELF IS REWRITTEN, not merely annotated:
// `required_floor_claim_cpu_safety_limit_ms` / `_wall_safety_limit_ms` are limits COOPERATIVELY
// OBSERVED while interpreted execution keeps returning to `eval_expr`'s own stride poll. They
// are NOT hard bounds across an opaque host operation unless that operation separately carries
// a deadline-aware contract or a killable process boundary -- neither of which
// `compile_dag_rust_emit_check` has today.

// WHICH REACHABILITY ONE CLAIM HAS, closed and named rather than left implicit per-occurrence.
// A claim either stays inside `eval_expr`'s own poll loop for its whole duration
// (`CooperativelyPollable` -- the case the two safety limits above genuinely protect), or it
// crosses into a synchronous native `free_call.*` arm that never re-enters `eval_expr` while it
// runs (`OpaqueHostCallUnbounded`, naming the operation). These are exactly the two mechanisms
// `std.evaluation_budget` `evaluation_budget_opaque_host_call_note` and this module's own prior
// comment already described in prose; this type is what the ruling asked that prose become.
type ClaimPreemptionReachability =
    CooperativelyPollable
  | OpaqueHostCallUnbounded { operation: String }

// THE ONE CLOSED, NO-GROWTH POPULATION of opaque host operations grandfathered under a per-claim
// safety guarantee TODAY, and it is exhaustive rather than illustrative: `compile_dag_rust_emit_check`
// is the only native `free_call.*` arm this corpus has measured reaching CPU cost the safety poll
// cannot see (root_d_checkpoint_scalar_declared_arity_witness_holds, floor run 32301212975,
// 60317ms against a 5000ms `required_floor_claim_cpu_safety_limit_ms`). This is the population
// the §4b rung-drop below reads -- named by the operations actually found reaching an opaque host
// call, not by which CLAIMS happen to call them today, so the population does not grow by a claim
// being added or removed; it grows only when a DIFFERENT operation is found to share this shape,
// which is a new finding, not a declaration.
fn opaque_host_call_grandfather_population() -> List<String> {
  Cons {
    head: "compile_dag_rust_emit_check",
    tail: Empty {}
  }
}

fn string_in_grandfather_population(needle: String, population: List<String>) -> Bool {
  match population {
    Empty {} => false
    Cons { head: h, tail: t } =>
      if needle == h {
        true
      } else {
        string_in_grandfather_population(needle: needle, population: t)
      }
  }
}

// THE MIGRATION WALL. A NEW opaque host operation brought under a per-claim safety guarantee
// must REFUSE (`false`, never a diagnostic that lets the run continue): nothing about the two
// safety-limit constants above makes an arbitrary native call deadline-aware, and admitting a
// second ungoverned operation by silence would repeat, for a second operation, the exact defect
// this file exists to name for the first. An EXISTING member of
// `opaque_host_call_grandfather_population` is admitted (`true`) with no growth to the
// population that admits it -- it derives `OpaqueHostCallUnbounded` rather than a fictitious
// hard-bound standing (see `claim_safety_outcome` below), never a refusal, because refusing it
// today would refuse the whole corpus of claims that already, honestly, exercise it.
fn claim_preemption_admission(preemption: ClaimPreemptionReachability) -> Bool {
  match preemption {
    CooperativelyPollable => true
    OpaqueHostCallUnbounded { operation: op } =>
      string_in_grandfather_population(needle: op, population: opaque_host_call_grandfather_population())
  }
}

// THE THREE-WAY OUTCOME THE RULING NAMED, replacing the binary `ClaimTerminality` this module's
// prose used to describe as though a third state did not need its own arm. `SafetyInterrupted`
// is reserved for a claim the poll genuinely stopped before a verdict -- it must never be the
// label for a claim that ran to completion, however far over its limit, because "terminal" and
// "interrupted" are different facts and conflating them is exactly the relabeling this ruling
// forbids. `CompletedPastSafetyLimit` names the other terminal-but-over-limit case and carries
// its `ClaimPreemptionReachability`, so a reader can tell WHY the poll never had a chance to fire
// without re-deriving it from a comment.
type ClaimSafetyOutcome =
    CompletedWithinSafetyLimits
  | SafetyInterrupted {
      raised_by: String
      elapsed_cpu_at_least: Int
      elapsed_wall_at_least: Int
    }
  | CompletedPastSafetyLimit {
      observed_cpu: Int
      observed_wall: Int
      preemption: ClaimPreemptionReachability
    }

// FLAT-SCALAR MILLISECOND FIELDS, DECLARED AS A BOUNDED GAP RATHER THAN SILENTLY LEFT (review
// 53860 on gunbc#8584, BLOCKING on `observed_cpu_ms`/`observed_wall_ms`/`cpu_limit_ms`/
// `wall_limit_ms` and the `ClaimSafetyOutcome` fields derived from them). The finding is correct
// on the merits and matches this repository's own sibling precedent: `std.evaluation_budget`
// (the caller-agnostic kernel this module's constants already cite) carries the identical
// domain -- a cooperative deadline observed in milliseconds -- through `std.measure`
// `Nanosecond`/`PositiveMillisecond`, never a bare `_ms: Int`. It is not fixed inline here
// because `std.measure` `Millisecond = Measure<Time, Milli, std.nat.Nat>` is built on v1's
// host-backed `Nat`, while this module's own `Int`/`Nat` (`v2.std.integer`, `v2.std.nat`) are
// v2's inductive Peano tower (`Nat = Zero | Succ { prev: Nat }`, verified in
// `src/v2/std/nat.dag`) -- and no bridge between the two numeric towers is established anywhere
// in this corpus today. Converting these four fields under an unrelated CI-fix pass would mean
// authoring that bridge for the first time, unverified, exactly the class of silent
// cross-representation mismatch this repository's own incident history warns against (a prior
// PR read cleanly and mismatched a carrier seal at runtime). Recorded as a typed obligation
// rather than a prose promise so it cannot decay into an unmarked scaffold.
data flat_scalar_millisecond_fields_dissolve_on: DissolutionCondition = unbound_dissolution(
  description: "dissolve-on: ClaimSafetyOutcome's observed_cpu/observed_wall/elapsed_cpu_at_least/elapsed_wall_at_least and claim_safety_outcome's observed_cpu_ms/observed_wall_ms/cpu_limit_ms/wall_limit_ms parameters convert from bare Int to std.measure Millisecond once a verified Int/Nat conversion exists between v2's inductive Peano tower (v2.std.integer.Int = GroupCompletion<v2.std.nat.Nat>) and the host-backed Nat std.measure is built on (per review 53860 on gunbc#8584)." as NonEmptyStr,
)

// THE DERIVATION. `reached_verdict` is the one fact that decides between `SafetyInterrupted` and
// the two completed arms -- never the observed costs, which is what keeps a claim that merely
// ran long from being misreported as interrupted, and a claim that was genuinely cut off from
// being misreported as complete. `preemption` and `raised_by` are supplied by the caller (the
// reachability fact and the trigger are observed at the interpreter boundary this module does
// not itself reach into) rather than guessed here.
fn claim_safety_outcome(
  reached_verdict: Bool,
  observed_cpu_ms: Int,
  observed_wall_ms: Int,
  cpu_limit_ms: Int,
  wall_limit_ms: Int,
  raised_by: String,
  preemption: ClaimPreemptionReachability,
) -> ClaimSafetyOutcome {
  match reached_verdict {
    false =>
      SafetyInterrupted {
        raised_by: raised_by,
        elapsed_cpu_at_least: observed_cpu_ms,
        elapsed_wall_at_least: observed_wall_ms,
      }
    true =>
      if int_gt(a: observed_cpu_ms, b: cpu_limit_ms) {
        CompletedPastSafetyLimit {
          observed_cpu: observed_cpu_ms,
          observed_wall: observed_wall_ms,
          preemption: preemption,
        }
      } else {
        if int_gt(a: observed_wall_ms, b: wall_limit_ms) {
          CompletedPastSafetyLimit {
            observed_cpu: observed_cpu_ms,
            observed_wall: observed_wall_ms,
            preemption: preemption,
          }
        } else {
          CompletedWithinSafetyLimits
        }
      }
  }
}

// §4b RUNG DROP, DECLARED (operator-directed, 2026-08-19; PREEMPTION-1).
//
// PREVIOUS CLAIMED RUNG: mechanically preventable -- the two safety-limit constants above were
// described (in this module's own prior prose and in `ClaimTerminality`'s Rust doc comment) as
// though arming them made ANY claim's cost, wherever it accrued, reliably interruptible.
//
// ACTUAL CURRENT RUNG: mitigatable, and only for the `CooperativelyPollable` arm of
// `ClaimPreemptionReachability`. For a claim whose cost accrues inside an
// `OpaqueHostCallUnbounded` operation the two limits are not even mitigatable -- they observe
// nothing and block nothing at the moment the cost accrues; the harm is caught only after the
// fact, once the claim completes, through `RequiredFloorOutcome.completed_over_cost_requirement`
// (a `CompletedPastSafetyLimit` occurrence, per `claim_safety_outcome_blocks` below, blocking
// exactly as a genuine interrupt would).
//
// BOUNDED POPULATION, DERIVED FROM HOST-OPERATION INVOCATION REACH, NOT HAND-LISTED BY CLAIM
// IDENTITY: `opaque_host_call_grandfather_population()` above. It is a population of OPERATIONS,
// not of claims -- naming `root_d_checkpoint_scalar_declared_arity_witness_holds` here would be
// exactly the hand-authored, non-derived population the ruling rejected, because the next claim
// to reach `compile_dag_rust_emit_check` would be uncovered by a list keyed on identity rather
// than on the operation it reaches. Any claim reaching a listed operation is, by that reach
// alone, in the dropped population; `claim_preemption_admission` is the decidable membership
// test over it, and it is exhaustive today at exactly one member.
//
// RESTORATION TRIGGER: this rung restores to mechanically preventable for the
// `OpaqueHostCallUnbounded` population when `compile_dag_rust_emit_check` (or any future member
// of the grandfather population) gains a deadline-aware contract or runs behind a killable
// process/worker boundary -- per `ClaimPreemptionReachability`'s own doc comment and
// v1_interpreter's `eval_expr` comment naming worker isolation, not a budget, as what would bound
// it unconditionally. Until then this rung is the honest one, and S2 (building that boundary) is
// out of scope for this model.
//
// BINDING CONSTRAINT: NO AMOUNT OF LATER COMPLETION CONVERTS A MISSED INTERRUPT INTO SUCCESSFUL
// ENFORCEMENT. `CompletedPastSafetyLimit` blocks exactly as `SafetyInterrupted` does -- a
// consumer that only checks for `SafetyInterrupted` before deciding admission reproduces the
// silent-completion defect this whole model exists to close. This predicate is the one place
// that fact is asserted, so a caller cannot rediscover it (or get it wrong) per call site.
fn claim_safety_outcome_blocks(outcome: ClaimSafetyOutcome) -> Bool {
  match outcome {
    CompletedWithinSafetyLimits => false
    SafetyInterrupted { raised_by: _, elapsed_cpu_at_least: _, elapsed_wall_at_least: _ } => true
    CompletedPastSafetyLimit { observed_cpu: _, observed_wall: _, preemption: _ } => true
  }
}

// ── SHARED PREPARATION BUILDS: A SECOND UNIT OF COST, WITH ITS OWN WALL ───────────────────
//
// THE LAW THIS SECTION ENCODES: THE COMPUTATION THAT FIRST TRIGGERS A SHARED ARTIFACT IS NOT
// THE COMPUTATION THAT OWNS ITS COST. First touch is PROVENANCE; the artifact's reuse boundary
// decides attribution. A floor run's cost is therefore TWO terms with INDEPENDENT blocking
// outcomes -- `PreparationOrSharedBuildCost + ClaimMarginalCost` -- and not one scalar the
// per-claim limits above may be asked to answer for.
//
// WHY IT IS DECLARED RATHER THAN LEFT AS A HOST DETAIL. The bare-reference edge index
// (`v1_compiler.cli_run` `both_closure_edge_index`, and through it `tree_bare_census_for_root`)
// is memoized once per index -- the census trace reports two misses over two source roots
// against ONE index address, `edge_index_construction { builds: 1 }` -- so its ~28s is neither
// duplicated nor repeated. It was simply BILLED to whichever claim resolved an entry first:
// `test.claim.qualified_spelling_identity_witness_test.qualified_spelling_takes_the_shared_layer`
// at 57193ms against a 5000ms `required_floor_claim_cpu_safety_limit_ms`, while its sibling
// reaching the identical computation milliseconds later measured 5ms. Three earlier modules
// were quarantined down exactly this chain before the pattern was read correctly
// (docs/plans/witness-cost-first-touch-attribution.md), each read as a slow test and each in
// fact the same one-time build landing on whoever touched it first.
//
// WHAT THIS IS NOT. It is NOT a cost reduction and must not be cited as one: the same work
// happens once either way and total run wall is unchanged. It is also NOT accounting
// laundering -- moving the work outside the claim timer while leaving it merely informational
// would mean no row blocks, the real cost still occurs, and nothing owns the refusal. THE
// SAFETY WALL MOVES WITH THE COST, which is what the three limits and the refusal below are.
//
// NO PRORATION, EVER. A shared build's cost is never divided across the claims that consume
// the artifact: a per-row fraction changes whenever the roster changes, which would make a
// row's admissibility depend on how many unrelated consumers happen to be enrolled.

// The shared builds a floor run pays before any per-claim timer starts. A CLOSED population --
// each is an artifact this repository has measured being charged to a first toucher, and the
// list grows only when another such artifact is FOUND, never by declaration.
type FloorPreparationPhase =
    ModulePathIndexBuild
  | SharedModuleIndexBuild
  | BareReferenceEdgeIndexBuild
  | LanguagesConsumerCensusBuild

// The terminal disposition of ONE shared preparation build. `PreparationWithinLimits` carries
// its observations too: a phase that reports nothing is indistinguishable from a phase that did
// not run, and this population's whole defect history is cost that nobody was reporting.
type FloorPreparationOutcome =
    PreparationWithinLimits {
      phase: FloorPreparationPhase
      observed_cpu: Millisecond
      observed_wall: Millisecond
      observed_rss_growth: ByteSize
    }
  | FloorPreparationRefused {
      phase: FloorPreparationPhase
      observed_cpu: Millisecond
      observed_wall: Millisecond
      observed_rss_growth: ByteSize
    }

// THREE INDEPENDENT LIMITS FOR A DIFFERENT UNIT, and the reason they are not the claim numbers
// is not that 5000ms is too small -- it is that 5000ms answers a DIFFERENT QUESTION. The claim
// limits bound one witness's marginal evaluation; a shared build is one whole-corpus artifact
// that every claim consumes. Reusing the claim figure here would refuse every healthy run, and
// raising the claim figure to admit a shared build would admit the defect rather than measure
// it -- the exact exit the quarantine row's dissolution trigger already forbids.
//
// EACH LIMIT IS DERIVED FROM THE RESOURCE IT PROTECTS, NOT FROM WHAT THE BUILD CURRENTLY COSTS.
// That is what makes the claim limits legitimate -- `gunbc.witness_row_cost`
// `witness_row_cost_migration_threshold_note` calls 5000ms "a FAIL-STOP PROTECTING THE EXECUTOR",
// "never a budget, tolerance, or target" -- and it is the only derivation available here, because
// measured-max-plus-headroom is the oracle the 2026-08-01 operator ruling names directly: a
// measurement copied from the same current tree is not an oracle, and automating its update
// collapses the assertion to `measure() == measure()`. A resource-derived line stays true when
// the build gets faster OR slower; a measurement-derived one silently ratifies whatever the tree
// happens to do today. The measured cold build is reported beside these numbers in the run and in
// this section's opening receipt -- it is evidence ABOUT the build, never the source of its ceiling.
//
// TEMPORARY RESTORATION ENVELOPE, exactly as the two claim limits above are, and stated as such
// rather than as re-founded permanent ceilings; the values below are PROPOSED FOR OPERATOR
// RATIFICATION with their derivation visible, so a different ruling changes only the literal and
// none of the structure.

// THE RESOURCE: the CI job budget. `gunbc.witness_floor_workflow` declares the witnesses job at
// `timeout_minutes: 180` -- 10,800,000ms -- and a job that exceeds it is killed by the runner with
// no ledger at all, which is the harm this limit exists to preempt with a typed, located refusal
// instead. THE POLICY: no single shared preparation build may consume more than ONE TENTH of that
// budget. The fraction is the population's own arithmetic rather than a taste: `FloorPreparationPhase`
// is closed at four members, so even all four at their ceiling leave 60% of the job for the claim
// fold that is the run's actual product.
//
// THE CLOCK BASIS IS CPU, AND IT IS CHOSEN BY PURPOSE, never by whichever number read higher
// (`required_floor_cost_basis`, and the same rule the claim pair follows): a shared preparation
// build is pure in-process work on one thread, so CPU is what a runaway shows up on.
fn required_floor_preparation_cpu_safety_limit() -> Millisecond {
  millisecond(count: 1080000)
}

// The SAME resource and the same tenth, read on the wall clock and then deliberately LOOSENED to a
// fifth of the job budget, for the reason the claim pair is split: a preparation build can be
// legitimately descheduled by a co-tenant self-hosted runner for stretches that would wrongly read
// as a runaway if judged on the CPU-tight figure. Never a mechanical copy of the CPU figure -- the
// operator named "DO NOT set CPU and wall to the same 5000ms" as exactly the move to stop making.
fn required_floor_preparation_wall_safety_limit() -> Millisecond {
  millisecond(count: 2160000)
}

// THE THIRD AXIS EXISTS BECAUSE THE FIRST TWO CANNOT SEE IT. A shared build's characteristic harm
// is not slowness but RESIDENCY: it is a corpus-scale structure retained for the whole run, and the
// floor's own worst historical memory events are exactly this shape (one row `grew rss by 1.00GB`).
// A build that stays inside both clocks while retaining gigabytes still endangers the executor.
//
// THE RESOURCE: the host memory cap, WITH SWAP DISABLED -- so exceeding it is an immediate kill, not
// a spill -- and shared, so the kernel OOM-kills the LARGEST task in the slice when the AGGREGATE is
// exceeded. A process can therefore be killed well under its own cap because of a peer, and the
// quantity that decides whether it is the victim is its own size relative to its co-tenants. THE
// POLICY: one shared preparation build may not grow the process by more than ONE TENTH of the 31 GiB
// cap the execution environment declares -- 3 GiB, stated in bytes below. Beyond that a single
// preparation phase is bidding to be the largest task in a shared slice, which is the condition for
// being selected for the kill, and it is the same authority that already forbids whole-tree local
// builds outright.
fn required_floor_preparation_rss_growth_limit() -> ByteSize {
  byte_size(count: 3221225472)
}

// THE DERIVATION. Any one axis crossing refuses the phase -- the three limits are independent
// bounds on one unit, never tiers to be ordered against each other. There is no warn arm and no
// widen arm: DESIGN §5 rules that a failure arm must refuse rather than widen, and a shared
// build that "ran long but was allowed to continue" would be an observation with no remedy
// attached, which is what the deleted per-claim warn tier already cost this module once.
fn floor_preparation_outcome(
  phase: FloorPreparationPhase,
  observed_cpu: Millisecond,
  observed_wall: Millisecond,
  observed_rss_growth: ByteSize,
  cpu_limit: Millisecond,
  wall_limit: Millisecond,
  rss_growth_limit: ByteSize,
) -> FloorPreparationOutcome {
  if millisecond_count(m: observed_cpu) > millisecond_count(m: cpu_limit) {
    FloorPreparationRefused {
      phase: phase,
      observed_cpu: observed_cpu,
      observed_wall: observed_wall,
      observed_rss_growth: observed_rss_growth,
    }
  } else {
    if millisecond_count(m: observed_wall) > millisecond_count(m: wall_limit) {
      FloorPreparationRefused {
        phase: phase,
        observed_cpu: observed_cpu,
        observed_wall: observed_wall,
        observed_rss_growth: observed_rss_growth,
      }
    } else {
      if byte_size_count(b: observed_rss_growth) > byte_size_count(b: rss_growth_limit) {
        FloorPreparationRefused {
          phase: phase,
          observed_cpu: observed_cpu,
          observed_wall: observed_wall,
          observed_rss_growth: observed_rss_growth,
        }
      } else {
        PreparationWithinLimits {
          phase: phase,
          observed_cpu: observed_cpu,
          observed_wall: observed_wall,
          observed_rss_growth: observed_rss_growth,
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

// A REFUSED PREPARATION STOPS THE RUN, and this predicate is the one place that is asserted so
// no caller rediscovers it. The consequence for the claims is the part that must not be got
// wrong: they are NOT EXECUTED, and a run that refuses here must produce no per-witness sheet at
// all. Reporting a roster of zero-cost claims beside a refused preparation would be a fabricated
// plausible output -- rows that read as measured and passing while nothing ever ran them.
fn floor_preparation_outcome_blocks(outcome: FloorPreparationOutcome) -> Bool {
  match outcome {
    PreparationWithinLimits { phase: _, observed_cpu: _, observed_wall: _, observed_rss_growth: _ } => false
    FloorPreparationRefused { phase: _, observed_cpu: _, observed_wall: _, observed_rss_growth: _ } => true
  }
}

// TRIGGERED_BY IS NOT OWNED_BY, AND THE DISTINCTION IS STRUCTURAL HERE RATHER THAN PROSE.
//
// This is the law of the whole change stated as a type. The computation that first TRIGGERS a
// shared artifact is not the computation that OWNS its cost; the artifact's reuse boundary decides
// ownership. A `Bool` saying "it was already warm" cannot carry that: it records that someone else
// went first while discarding WHO, which is exactly the fact an attribution model exists to keep.
//
// `BuiltByPreparation` is the intended state -- trigger and owner COINCIDE, which is what the warm
// buys. `AlreadyWarmOnEntry` says preparation found the artifact built and names the phase or call
// site that got there first; that name is PROVENANCE and never an assignment of cost. A run
// reporting it is reporting a defect in ordering, not a claim about who should pay.
type SharedBuildProvenance =
    BuiltByPreparation
  | AlreadyWarmOnEntry { triggered_by: String }

// THE TWO TERMS, AND THEY ARE SUMMED FOR REPORTING ONLY, NEVER FOR ADMISSION. A floor run's cost is
// `preparation_or_shared_build + claim_marginal` -- one term per UNIT OF ATTRIBUTION, not per phase
// of execution. The split exists so neither term can be judged by the other's limits: the per-claim
// safety deadlines answer a claim-level question and a whole-corpus shared build is a different
// unit with its own resource-derived ceilings.
//
// NO PRORATION IS EXPRESSIBLE HERE, and that is structural rather than a rule someone must follow:
// there is no per-claim share of the preparation term to compute, because the term is not indexed
// by claim. A per-row fraction would change whenever the roster changes, making a row's
// admissibility depend on how many unrelated consumers happen to be enrolled.
type FloorRunCost {
  preparation_or_shared_build: Millisecond
  claim_marginal: Millisecond
}

// INDEPENDENT BLOCKING OUTCOMES, ASSERTED IN ONE PLACE SO NO CALLER RE-DERIVES IT. Either term can
// block on its own and neither subsumes the other: a shared build past its ceiling refuses even
// when every claim is healthy, and a claim past its deadline refuses even when preparation was
// well inside its envelope. The disjunction is what "independent" means operationally -- this is
// NOT a precedence rule, and there is deliberately no arm in which one outcome excuses the other.
fn floor_run_blocks(
  preparation: FloorPreparationOutcome,
  claim: ClaimSafetyOutcome,
) -> Bool {
  floor_preparation_outcome_blocks(outcome: preparation)
    || claim_safety_outcome_blocks(outcome: claim)
}

// §4b RUNG, DECLARED AT THE HONEST GRAIN. The model above is *mechanically preventable* only
// where a caller actually consults it. The host (`v1_compiler.cli_run` `run_required_floor`)
// reads the three limits from this module -- so the NUMBERS have a single authority and cannot
// be re-spelled in Rust -- but MIRRORS the comparison and the refusal by hand rather than
// evaluating `floor_preparation_outcome` through the frame. That mirror is *mitigatable*: a
// divergence between the two is writable and nothing detects it. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER: the host
// calling this derivation through the hermetic frame it already holds, at which point the
// mirrored branch is deleted rather than kept beside it (§4b(4) -- dissolution on climb).
data floor_preparation_host_mirror_dissolve_on: DissolutionCondition = unbound_dissolution(
  description: "dissolve-on: v1_compiler.cli_run run_required_floor evaluates floor_preparation_outcome / floor_preparation_outcome_blocks through the hermetic frame instead of mirroring the three-axis comparison in Rust; the mirrored branch is deleted in the same change." as NonEmptyStr,
)
